/* Section 33.1 clinical case cards: single best answer. Source: Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry, 12th ed., Chapter 33 (The Human Brain; Functional Neuroanatomy). */
KS.add("ch33-1", "cases", [
  {"id":"c331-hb01","tag":"Brain and psychiatry","q":"A 35-year-old man feels well and has a normal examination. Routine blood work shows diagnostic fasting glucose values. His cousin asks whether a similar routine test could find schizophrenia before symptoms. Which statement best answers her?","choices":["Brain imaging is the accepted way to diagnose silent schizophrenia","An endophenotype is the accepted diagnostic marker for schizophrenia","Schizophrenia has a validated blood marker used for screening","A genetic test can confirm schizophrenia before symptoms appear","No objective, independent assessment for schizophrenia is currently recognized"],"answer":4,"why":"Unlike diabetes, schizophrenia cannot be diagnosed in a person without symptoms, because no objective, independent test is recognized (s331-brain)."},
  {"id":"c331-hb02","tag":"Brain and psychiatry","q":"A 29-year-old woman has both persistent low mood and chronic worry. One clinician labels her with major depressive disorder, another with generalized anxiety disorder, and both prescribe the same drug. A resident asks what this pattern suggests. Which is the best answer?","choices":["Drug response may reflect brain dysfunction better than symptom groups","The drug must be acting as a placebo in one disorder","DSM-5 categories for these two disorders are fully valid","Only one of the clinicians has applied the manual correctly","The two disorders have been proven to be one gene"],"answer":0,"why":"The chapter notes these conditions overlap and share treatments, suggesting drug response may be a better guide to underlying biology than symptom clusters (s331-brain)."},
  {"id":"c331-hb03","tag":"Brain and psychiatry","q":"A 50-year-old man with posttraumatic symptoms is helped by propranolol, and a colleague's patient with a psychiatric disorder improves on verapamil. A student tries to sort these drugs into the four classic classes. What point does the chapter make about them?","choices":["They act only on nonpsychiatric symptoms","They are best grouped with the antipsychotics","They are best grouped with the mood stabilizers","They are effective but fit none of the four classic classes","They are antidepressants with unusual side effects"],"answer":3,"why":"Clonidine, propranolol and verapamil treat several psychiatric disorders yet do not fit the antipsychotic, antidepressant, antianxiety or mood-stabilizer classes (s331-brain)."},
  {"id":"c331-hb04","tag":"Brain and psychiatry","q":"A drug company screens new compounds only in a mouse test that was built to detect tricyclic antidepressants. Every hit raises serotonin levels. What is the main risk of this strategy, as the chapter describes it?","choices":["The test will find drugs with new mechanisms too often","The test may detect a molecular mechanism rather than a true analog of illness","Mice cannot express serotonin receptors","The strategy is guaranteed to miss all antidepressants","The test overestimates side effects in humans"],"answer":1,"why":"Models built around existing drugs may only detect a shared mechanism such as raising serotonin, not a real behavioral analog such as despair in depression (s331-brain)."},
  {"id":"c331-hb05","tag":"Brain and psychiatry","q":"A research team finds the same pattern of working memory deficits and imaging changes in some patients with schizophrenia, some with bipolar disorder and some with depression. The pattern is measured objectively and is not visible on examination. What is this pattern best called?","choices":["An endophenotype","A diagnostic marker","A syndrome","A presenting symptom","A comorbidity"],"answer":0,"why":"An endophenotype is an objective internal trait set not visible to the unaided eye; it can cross diagnoses and is neither a symptom nor a diagnostic marker (s331-brain)."},
  {"id":"c331-hb06","tag":"Brain and psychiatry","q":"A 42-year-old woman with chronic stress develops recurrent infections and menstrual irregularity. Her psychiatrist explains that brain, hormone and immune systems signal one another. Which field studies the brain and immune interaction?","choices":["Neurogenetics","Psychoneuroimmunology","Chronobiology","Psychopharmacology","Psychoneuroendocrinology"],"answer":1,"why":"Recognizing that the CNS communicates with the immune system gave rise to psychoneuroimmunology; the endocrine link is psychoneuroendocrinology (s331-brain)."},
  {"id":"c331-hg01","tag":"Genes and diagnosis","q":"A 19-year-old man with schizophrenia has an identical twin brother who remains well at age 30. The parents ask what this means. Which explanation best fits the chapter?","choices":["A single gene of large effect must be absent in one twin","Identical twins always share the same psychiatric disorders","The diagnosis in the affected twin must be wrong","Schizophrenia has no genetic component","Genes interact with the environment to shape the outcome"],"answer":4,"why":"Discordance of identical twins for schizophrenia supports many significant interactions between genome and environment (s331-genome)."},
  {"id":"c331-hg02","tag":"Genes and diagnosis","q":"A family asks whether one gene caused their son's bipolar disorder. The psychiatrist summarizes the current view in the chapter. Which is the most accurate statement?","choices":["The disorder is fully explained by environment","One gene of large effect causes most cases","Genes play no role in the disorder","Genetic testing can identify the single responsible gene","Several genes, each with a modest effect, likely contribute"],"answer":4,"why":"Evidence suggests any single gene has only a modest effect and a disorder reflects multiple genes, speculatively five to ten, though some researchers still expect large-effect genes (s331-genome)."},
  {"id":"c331-hg03","tag":"Genes and diagnosis","q":"A first-year resident writes a formulation covering the patient's brain abnormalities, unconscious conflicts and family and cultural setting, noting how each influences the others. Whose model is she applying?","choices":["Sigmund Freud","James Papez","George Engel","Emil Kraepelin","Adolf Meyer"],"answer":2,"why":"George Engel's 1977 biopsychosocial model integrates biologic, psychological and social systems that affect one another (s331-genome)."},
  {"id":"c331-hg04","tag":"Genes and diagnosis","q":"At a journal club, a speaker proposes replacing DSM categories with a fully genetic diagnostic system right away. Which response best reflects the chapter?","choices":["It is already standard in most countries","It is unneeded because DSM categories are proven valid","It is impossible because mental disorders are not heritable","It is premature given genetic complexity, limited data and environmental effects","It is ideal because single genes cause most disorders"],"answer":3,"why":"The chapter calls a completely genetic system premature because of complex genetics, insufficient data and the weight of environmental and epigenetic effects (s331-genome)."},
  {"id":"c331-hg05","tag":"Genes and diagnosis","q":"A researcher studies how a risk gene changes a patient's brain function. According to the chapter, at what level should the gene's effects be sought?","choices":["In axonal projections, synaptic integrity and intraneuronal signaling","Only in the patient's reported symptoms","Only in the DSM category assigned","Only in the size of the whole brain","Only in the amount of protein in blood"],"answer":0,"why":"Downstream effects of abnormal genes are changes in discrete features such as synaptic integrity, axonal projections and intraneuronal signaling steps within circuits (s331-genome)."},
  {"id":"c331-hg06","tag":"Genes and diagnosis","q":"A student says personality is purely learned and genes cannot explain it. Which figure from human research best answers him?","choices":["About 5 to 10 percent is genetic","About 90 to 100 percent is genetic","About 40 to 70 percent is genetic","No genetic share has been estimated","About 10 to 20 percent is genetic"],"answer":2,"why":"Human research generally links about 40 to 70 percent of features of temperament, cognition and personality to genetic factors (s331-genome)."},
  {"id":"c331-hn01","tag":"Lessons from neurology","q":"A 38-year-old man has depression, then later obsessive symptoms and psychosis, each diagnosed separately. He then develops chorea, and testing confirms an autosomal dominant trinucleotide repeat disorder. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alzheimer disease","Lewy body dementia","Huntington disease","Multiple sclerosis","Parkinson disease"],"answer":2,"why":"Huntington disease is autosomal dominant, involves trinucleotide repeats and carries high risk of many psychiatric syndromes and DSM-5 diagnoses (s331-neuro)."},
  {"id":"c331-hn02","tag":"Lessons from neurology","q":"A 72-year-old woman with fluctuating cognition and parkinsonism is diagnosed with Lewy body dementia. A student asks what biologic group this disease belongs to. Which is correct?","choices":["Alpha-synucleinopathies","Trinucleotide repeat disorders","Demyelinating disorders","Autosomal dominant prion disorders","Amyloid precursor protein disorders"],"answer":0,"why":"The chapter lists Parkinson disease and Lewy body dementia as alpha-synucleinopathies (s331-neuro)."},
  {"id":"c331-hn03","tag":"Lessons from neurology","q":"A 66-year-old man develops low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest three months after an ischemic stroke. His neurologist asks whether this belongs to psychiatry or neurology. Which view does the chapter support?","choices":["It is purely a neurologic complication","The division between the fields is arbitrary since both involve the brain","It is purely a psychiatric disorder unrelated to the stroke","It should be managed only after the stroke resolves fully","Depression never follows stroke"],"answer":1,"why":"The chapter cites post-stroke depression as overlap and calls the psychiatry and neurology division arbitrary, since both share the same organ (s331-neuro)."},
  {"id":"c331-hn04","tag":"Lessons from neurology","q":"A 28-year-old man with schizophrenia shows abnormal involuntary movements. A student asks what this illustrates in the chapter's argument. What is the best answer?","choices":["Psychiatric disorders never involve the motor system","Severe psychiatric disorders can have neurologic symptoms","The finding proves a single causative gene","Movement disorders exclude a diagnosis of schizophrenia","The movements must be unrelated to his illness"],"answer":1,"why":"The chapter cites movement disorders in schizophrenia as an example of neurologic symptoms in severe psychiatric disorders (s331-neuro)."},
  {"id":"c331-hn05","tag":"Lessons from neurology","q":"A 30-year-old woman with paranoid ideas misreads neutral sounds as voices. Her supervisor reminds the resident that psychiatry focuses on association function. Why is knowing sensory processing still essential here?","choices":["Sensory systems are the main seat of thought","It helps separate logical thought from distortions of psychopathology","Motor units create the internal map of the world","Sensory maps are shared by all modalities","Association units have no role in perception"],"answer":1,"why":"Understanding sensory and motor processing is essential to tell logical thought from distortions introduced by psychopathology; each modality has its own map (s331-neuro)."},
  {"id":"c331-bo01","tag":"Brain organization","q":"A neuropathologist examines a brain biopsy from a 45-year-old man with multiple sclerosis-like lesions in the spinal cord. She wants to identify the cells that normally produce myelin in that tissue. Which cell type is she looking for?","choices":["Microglia","Schwann cells","Oligodendrocytes","Dendrites","Astrocytes"],"answer":2,"why":"In the CNS, including the spinal cord, myelin comes from oligodendrocytes; Schwann cells myelinate peripheral axons (s331-org, glial table)."},
  {"id":"c331-bo02","tag":"Brain organization","q":"A 70-year-old woman dies weeks after a large ischemic stroke. Autopsy shows many cells engulfing the debris of dead neurons in the infarct. These cells derive from macrophages. Which glial type are they?","choices":["Astrocytes","Pyramidal neurons","Schwann cells","Oligodendrocytes","Microglia"],"answer":4,"why":"Microglia are derived from macrophages and remove cellular debris after neuronal death (s331-org)."},
  {"id":"c331-bo03","tag":"Brain organization","q":"A 38-year-old man with diabetes has nerve conduction slowing in his feet. A resident asks which glial cell myelinates these peripheral nerve fibers. What is the best answer?","choices":["Microglia","Oligodendrocytes","Schwann cells","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells"],"answer":2,"why":"Schwann cells wrap axons in the peripheral nervous system to form myelin that speeds conduction (s331-org)."},
  {"id":"c331-bo04","tag":"Brain organization","q":"A researcher studying a 30-year-old donor brain wants to compare the thickness of cortical layers in two regions by showing the size and packing of cell bodies. Which method best fits?","choices":["Axonal tracer injection","Serotonin assay","Myelin stain of fiber tracts","Nissl stain","Blood-brain barrier dye"],"answer":3,"why":"Nissl stains, aniline dyes that bind ribonucleotides in cell bodies, reveal cytoarchitecture: neuron size, packing density and cortical layers (s331-org)."},
  {"id":"c331-bo05","tag":"Brain organization","q":"A 25-year-old medical student asks why a sedative drug can reach the brain while many toxins cannot. Her preceptor names the glial cell that integrates with the blood-brain barrier and also nourishes neurons. Which cell did he name?","choices":["Microglia","Soma","Oligodendrocyte","Astrocyte","Schwann cell"],"answer":3,"why":"Astrocytes, the most common glia, nourish neurons, inactivate some transmitters and integrate with the BBB (s331-org)."},
  {"id":"c331-so01","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"A 66-year-old right-handed man has a small right parietal stroke. With eyes closed he cannot identify a coin placed in his left hand, but light touch, pain, temperature, vibration and joint position in that hand are normal. What is the most likely deficit?","choices":["Pressure palsy","Thalamic sensory loss","Tactile agnosia (astereognosis)","Peripheral neuropathy","Conversion disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"Failure to recognize objects by touch with intact primary modalities defines tactile agnosia, localized to the posterior parietal somatosensory-association border (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-so02","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"A 52-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes reports burning pain, tingling and numbness in both feet. Examination of mental status is normal. Where along the somatosensory pathway is the problem most likely?","choices":["Peripheral sensory nerve axons","Barrel cortex","Thalamus","Corticothalamic return fibers","Posterior parietal association cortex"],"answer":0,"why":"Peripheral neuropathy usually presents with numbness, tingling and pain; the long sensory axons are vulnerable to systemic medical conditions (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-so03","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"A 24-year-old woman develops loss of all sensation on the left half of her body after a family conflict. Imaging and nerve studies are normal. Which mechanism does the chapter propose for this kind of syndrome?","choices":["Damage to peripheral mechanoreceptors","A lesion of the posterior parietal association cortex","Loss of oligodendrocyte myelin","Failure of barrel formation","Cortical interference through reciprocal corticothalamic fibers"],"answer":4,"why":"Reciprocal cortex-to-thalamus fibers can inappropriately suppress sensation; this is thought to underlie hemisensory loss in conversion disorder (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-so04","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"A 58-year-old man loses sensation in his hand after a stroke damaging part of his somatosensory cortex. Months later sensation has improved though the infarct is unchanged on imaging. What best explains the recovery?","choices":["Regrowth of the destroyed cortex","The body map contracting and shifting into intact cortex","Penfield's fixed homunculus","New peripheral receptors forming in the skin","Increased cortical serotonin"],"answer":1,"why":"After a stroke ablates part of the homunculus, the map shrinks and shifts to fill the remaining cortex; this plasticity allows recovery (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-so05","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"A neuroscientist breeds mice with fewer whiskers than normal. Compared with normal mice, what will the somatosensory barrel field most likely show?","choices":["More barrels, each smaller","Fewer barrels, each larger, same total area","Fewer barrels, each normal, smaller total area","Same number of barrels, each smaller","No barrels at all"],"answer":1,"why":"Fewer whiskers give fewer barrels, each expanded, with the field covering the same area as normal (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-so06","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"A laboratory studies mutant mice that lack monoamine oxidase A. Which finding in the somatosensory cortex does the chapter describe?","choices":["Thalamic relay is bypassed","Barrels fail to form","Myelin sheaths are absent","The homunculus rotates 90 degrees","Barrels are doubled in number"],"answer":1,"why":"MAO A knockout mice have very high cortical serotonin and no barrels, indirectly implicating serotonin in barrel development (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-so07","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"In an experiment, adult volunteers have the distal segments of all fingers stimulated together while distal and proximal contact on each finger is kept separate. Over time, how does the cortical finger map change?","choices":["It moves to the opposite hemisphere","It shifts by 90 degrees so proximal and distal parts are no longer adjacent","It is unchanged, as Penfield's map is fixed","It splits into two separate homunculi","It shrinks to half its area"],"answer":1,"why":"The map gradually rotates 90 degrees to match the new tactile experience, and proximal and distal segments stop being contiguous (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-so08","tag":"Somatosensory system","q":"A 40-year-old woman has a small thalamic hemorrhage and reduced sensation on one side. A student asks which sensory fibers pass through the thalamus. What is the best answer?","choices":["Only pain and temperature","Only fibers from the face","All somatosensory fibers","None; they go directly to cortex","Only proprioception"],"answer":2,"why":"Every somatosensory fiber relays in the thalamus, which keeps the body map and relays it to parietal cortex (s331-soma)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs01","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 72-year-old man recovering from cardiac arrest bumps into furniture yet insists he can see well and describes the room wrongly. Imaging shows damage to both occipital lobes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Prosopagnosia","Associative visual agnosia","Anton syndrome","Gerstmann syndrome","Balint syndrome"],"answer":2,"why":"Failure to acknowledge blindness with bilateral occipital lesions is Anton syndrome; hypoxic injury is a common cause (s331-vis, disorders table)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs02","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 58-year-old woman after bilateral strokes cannot reach accurately for a cup she can see, cannot move her gaze quickly on command, and sees only one item at a time in a picture. Where is the lesion most likely?","choices":["Bilateral parietooccipital cortex","Left inferior temporal cortex","Bilateral medial occipitotemporal cortex","Bilateral occipital poles with splenium","Dominant parietal lobe alone"],"answer":0,"why":"Optic ataxia, oculomotor apraxia and simultanagnosia form Balint syndrome, seen with bilateral parietooccipital lesions (s331-vis)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs03","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 64-year-old right-handed teacher after a stroke cannot write, makes simple arithmetic errors, confuses left and right, and cannot name which finger is touched. Where is the lesion most likely?","choices":["Bilateral visual association areas","Right parietal lobe","Dominant parietal lobe","Bilateral occipital lobes","Left medial temporal lobe"],"answer":2,"why":"Trouble writing, calculating, telling right from left and naming fingers make up Gerstmann syndrome, attributed to the dominant parietal lobe (s331-vis)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs04","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 70-year-old man cannot name a comb or show how to use it when he sees it, yet he copies a drawing of it accurately. Other senses are normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Associative visual agnosia","Simultanagnosia","Apperceptive visual agnosia","Anton syndrome","Color anomia"],"answer":0,"why":"Inability to name or use objects with preserved drawing is associative visual agnosia, from bilateral medial occipitotemporal lesions (s331-vis)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs05","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 55-year-old woman with bilateral visual association area damage cannot identify or copy simple objects by sight but names them at once when she touches them. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central achromatopsia","Apperceptive visual agnosia","Associative visual agnosia","Prosopagnosia","Balint syndrome"],"answer":1,"why":"Inability to identify and draw items by sight with other senses spared is apperceptive visual agnosia, from bilateral visual association area lesions (s331-vis)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs06","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 49-year-old man after a stroke cannot tell you what color a red card is and cannot point to red when asked, yet he correctly sorts the card with other red cards. Which is the best label?","choices":["Color anomia","Anton syndrome","Color agnosia","Central achromatopsia","Associative visual agnosia"],"answer":2,"why":"Inability to recognize a color while still able to match it is color agnosia; color anomia would spare pointing to a named color, and achromatopsia would abolish color perception (s331-vis)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs07","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 61-year-old woman recognizes her handbag, car and house but cannot recognize her husband's face until he speaks. Which mechanism does the chapter propose?","choices":["Disconnection of auditory cortex from Wernicke area","Bilateral occipital damage interrupting self-assessment fibers","Lesion of the dominant occipital lobe with splenium","Loss of ocular dominance columns","Left ITC cut off from the left parietal visual association area"],"answer":4,"why":"The chapter proposes that prosopagnosia arises when the left ITC is cut off from the left parietal visual association area (s331-vis). Later lesion studies link acquired prosopagnosia mainly to right or bilateral occipitotemporal damage (see the update box)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs08","tag":"Visual system","q":"An infant has one eye covered for a long period soon after birth. Years later the cortex is studied. What is the expected finding in primary visual cortex?","choices":["Both eyes' fibers remain intermixed","Loss of orientation columns only","Enlarged barrel fields","Fibers from the open eye occupy the entire visual cortex","Normal alternating ocular dominance columns"],"answer":3,"why":"Occluding one eye during the critical period removes its cortical fibers and lets the active eye innervate the whole visual cortex (s331-vis, development)."},
  {"id":"c331-vs09","tag":"Visual system","q":"A 45-year-old man with a right parietal tumor draws a house with good windows and door details but the outline and perspective are badly distorted. Which statement best explains this?","choices":["The left hemisphere supplies overall contour and perspective","The inferior temporal lobe tracks location and motion","Bilateral occipital damage causes denial of deficit","The right parietal lobe supplies contour, perspective and right-left orientation","Primary visual cortex supplies internal detail"],"answer":3,"why":"Outline, perspective and left-right orientation come mainly from the right parietal lobe, and inner detail from the left hemisphere (s331-vis)."},
  {"id":"c331-au01","tag":"Auditory system","q":"A 66-year-old man after a stroke hears a doorbell and recognizes a dog barking, but cannot understand spoken words, though he knows someone is talking. Which mechanism is proposed?","choices":["Right hemisphere damage to sound recognition","Damage to the medial geniculate nucleus only","Bilateral occipital lesions","Disconnection of auditory cortex from Wernicke area","Cochlear hair cell loss"],"answer":3,"why":"Intact hearing for voices with inability to recognize speech is word deafness, thought to disconnect auditory cortex from Wernicke area (s331-aud)."},
  {"id":"c331-au02","tag":"Auditory system","q":"A 59-year-old woman understands speech normally and passes a hearing test, but cannot say whether a sound is a car horn or a cat meowing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Gerstmann syndrome","Conductive hearing loss","Pure word deafness","Apperceptive visual agnosia","Auditory sound agnosia"],"answer":4,"why":"Inability to recognize nonverbal sounds with intact hearing and speech recognition is auditory sound agnosia, the right-sided counterpart of word deafness (s331-aud)."},
  {"id":"c331-au03","tag":"Auditory system","q":"A 7-year-old with speech delay and poor understanding of spoken language can tell speech sounds apart when a computer slows them. Which intervention does the chapter describe for such children?","choices":["Delaying language input until adolescence","Tutoring that starts with slowed phonemes and speeds up with correct answers","Training at a fixed normal speech rate","Hemispherectomy","Covering one ear during training"],"answer":1,"why":"The program began in a slowed voice and raised the phoneme rate as answers were correct, with gains over 2 to 6 weeks (s331-aud, development)."},
  {"id":"c331-au04","tag":"Auditory system","q":"A neurology resident presents a patient whose midbrain auditory relay is damaged. Which structure lies between the lateral lemniscus and the medial geniculate nucleus?","choices":["Cochlear nuclei","Nucleus solitarius","Inferior colliculi","Splenium","Pyriform cortex"],"answer":2,"why":"From the cochlear nuclei the signal climbs via the lateral lemniscus, reaches the inferior colliculi, then the MGN (s331-aud, pathway steps)."},
  {"id":"c331-au05","tag":"Auditory system","q":"A 9-year-old boy, mute because of intractable seizures in one hemisphere, has that hemisphere removed. What course does the chapter report in such a case?","choices":["Rapid language gains ending only a few years behind his age","Language returns only if the left side was kept","Language develops only in reading, not speech","No language ever develops","Full language appears within 2 weeks"],"answer":0,"why":"After removal at 9 years, the boy gained language quickly and ended only a few years delayed for his age (s331-aud, case)."},
  {"id":"c331-ol01","tag":"Smell, taste and hypnosis","q":"A 34-year-old veteran becomes suddenly distressed and has vivid memories when he smells diesel smoke. Which anatomic feature best explains the strength of this reaction?","choices":["Olfactory input passes directly to limbic structures without thalamic relay","Olfactory input reaches only the posterior parietal lobe","Olfactory input relays in the medial geniculate nucleus","Olfactory receptors are arranged tonotopically","Olfactory input enters the nucleus solitarius first"],"answer":0,"why":"Smell projects straight to the frontal lobe and limbic system (amygdala, hippocampus, pyriform cortex), skipping the thalamus, so odors evoke strong emotion and memory (s331-olf)."},
  {"id":"c331-ol02","tag":"Smell, taste and hypnosis","q":"A 28-year-old woman with a fracture through the cribriform plate after a car crash loses her sense of smell. Which part of the pathway is most likely disrupted?","choices":["Autonomic visceral fibers","Cochlear nerve fibers","Gustatory nerves to the nucleus solitarius","Sensory axons traveling to the olfactory bulb","Fibers of the splenium"],"answer":3,"why":"Olfactory sensory axons pass through the cribriform plate to reach the olfactory bulb (s331-olf, pathway)."},
  {"id":"c331-ol03","tag":"Smell, taste and hypnosis","q":"A 40-year-old volunteer under hypnosis eats an onion with pleasure as if it were a chocolate truffle, then rejects it seconds after the suggestion is reversed. Which statement best fits the chapter?","choices":["The sensory anatomy changes during hypnosis","Everyone can reach this hypnotic state","The same stimulus can carry opposite emotional value while anatomy is unchanged","The hypnotic switch is located in the nucleus solitarius","Changes take weeks to develop"],"answer":2,"why":"Hypnosis can instantly reverse the emotional value of a stimulus without changing sensory anatomy; the switch site is undetermined and only a proportion of people are hypnotizable (s331-olf)."},
  {"id":"c331-ol04","tag":"Smell, taste and hypnosis","q":"A 52-year-old man with a brainstem lesion has impaired taste. Which brainstem nucleus receives gustatory nerve input?","choices":["Cochlear nucleus","Inferior colliculus","Nucleus solitarius","Pyriform nucleus","Medial geniculate nucleus"],"answer":2,"why":"Taste (gustatory) nerves end in the brainstem's nucleus solitarius (s331-olf, taste)."},
  {"id":"c331-ol05","tag":"Smell, taste and hypnosis","q":"A 47-year-old woman with panic disorder struggles to describe her abdominal discomfort, calling it only an odd feeling. Which statement best explains this?","choices":["Autonomic fibers carry no information on blood pressure","Visceral input travels through the olfactory bulb","Visceral information reaches awareness rapidly and precisely","Most autonomic sensory information stays unconscious and is felt only vaguely","Visceral input is mapped tonotopically"],"answer":3,"why":"Autonomic sensory information is mostly unconscious and, if felt, is only a vague sensation (s331-olf, autonomic)."},
  {"id":"c331-ol06","tag":"Smell, taste and hypnosis","q":"In a lab study, young animals are raised exposed to a single strong scent after birth. What change in the olfactory bulb is expected?","choices":["Glomeruli number rises to 30,000","One glomerulus expands greatly at the expense of neighbors","The vomeronasal organ disappears","All glomeruli shrink equally","Odor signals reroute through the thalamus"],"answer":1,"why":"Early exposure to one dominant scent makes one glomerulus enlarge massively while surrounding glomeruli lose ground (s331-olf, development)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo01","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A 67-year-old man is seen two weeks after a large left cortical stroke. His right arm, initially limp, now resists passive stretch with increased tone. Which explanation best accounts for this change?","choices":["Regrowth of corticospinal axons into the arm","Brainstem motor systems released from cortical modulation","Overactivity of the vestibulospinal tract caused by the cortex","New cerebellar activation of agonist muscles","Degeneration of the substantia nigra"],"answer":1,"why":"Loss of cortical influence after stroke releases brainstem motor systems, and spasticity returns (Motor systems overview)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo02","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A pediatric resident examines a healthy 1-day-old girl whose arms and legs are held tightly flexed. Which tract is thought to dominate to produce this posture?","choices":["Corticospinal tract","Dorsal column pathway","Vestibulospinal tract","Callosal projection","Rubrospinal tract"],"answer":4,"why":"Rubrospinal activation flexes all limbs, and its dominance is presumed to explain newborn flexion (Motor systems overview)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo03","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A 41-year-old woman has progressive involuntary dancing movements, plus new paranoid psychosis. Her father had the same illness. Which structure is most expected to show marked shrinkage on imaging?","choices":["Caudate nucleus","Ventromedial hypothalamus","Globus pallidus","Subthalamic nucleus","Cerebellum"],"answer":0,"why":"The caudate shrinks dramatically in Huntington disease, which features chorea and possible psychosis (Basal ganglia)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo04","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A 70-year-old man has a lacunar infarct and suddenly develops violent, high-speed flinging movements of one arm. Which nucleus is most likely involved?","choices":["Subthalamic nucleus","Putamen","Red nucleus","Lateral hypothalamic nucleus","Caudate nucleus"],"answer":0,"why":"Subthalamic nucleus lesions produce ballistic movements, fast sudden limb jerks (Basal ganglia)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo05","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A 29-year-old man is found after carbon monoxide exposure. Weeks later he shows dystonic postures and flapping limb movements. Which structure is most likely damaged?","choices":["Caudate nucleus","Subthalamic nucleus","Globus pallidus","Cerebellar cortex","Supplementary motor area"],"answer":2,"why":"The globus pallidus can be severely damaged in carbon monoxide poisoning and Wilson disease, with dystonia and flapping movements (Basal ganglia)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo06","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A 64-year-old woman with tremor, rigidity and slow initiation of movement is referred for low mood. Which mechanism best explains her difficulty starting movements?","choices":["Ventromedial hypothalamic damage","Callosal disconnection of premotor areas","Striatal overactivity from loss of dopaminergic inhibition","Loss of rubrospinal flexor drive","Cerebellar failure to predict muscle contraction"],"answer":2,"why":"Striatal overactivity due to lack of dopaminergic inhibition, as in parkinsonian conditions, gives bradykinesia; depression affects over 30 percent (Basal ganglia)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo07","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A 15-year-old boy has motor and vocal tics and intrusive urges to repeat actions. Which basal ganglia function is thought to be failing?","choices":["Pallidal output to the thalamus","Cerebellar timing of agonist muscles","Caudate gatekeeping of goal-directed acts","Nigral melanin production","Subthalamic damping of limb velocity"],"answer":2,"why":"When the caudate fails as gatekeeper, extraneous acts appear, as in OCD and tic disorders such as Tourette disorder (Basal ganglia)."},
  {"id":"c331-mo08","tag":"Basal ganglia and cerebellum","q":"A 52-year-old man after resection of a cerebellar tumor reaches for a cup with jerky, shaky, poorly graded movement. Strength is normal. Which function has been lost?","choices":["Sequencing of multistep acts","Initiation of movement by dopamine","Flexion of all limbs","Comprehension of spoken commands","Prediction of agonist and antagonist contraction"],"answer":4,"why":"The cerebellum predicts the relative contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles; without it movements are coarse and tremulous (Cerebellum)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx01","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"A 58-year-old right-handed man cannot salute when asked, though he understands the request, has full strength, and salutes a passing officer without thinking. Which type of deficit is this?","choices":["Ideational apraxia","Ideomotor apraxia","Ballismus","Limb-kinetic apraxia","Bradykinesia"],"answer":1,"why":"Ideomotor apraxia is failure to perform an act on command with intact understanding, strength and spontaneous execution (Motor cortex)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx02","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"After surgery that cut the corpus callosum, a 34-year-old woman performs commanded gestures correctly with her right hand but not her left. Strength and understanding are intact. Where is the lesion responsible?","choices":["Right supplementary motor area","Left inferior temporal cortex","Subthalamic nucleus","Callosal fibers between the premotor areas","Right frontal lobe olfactory cortex"],"answer":3,"why":"The left premotor program crosses via the corpus callosum to reach right premotor cortex, which runs the left hand; a callosal lesion causes isolated left-hand ideomotor apraxia (Motor cortex)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx03","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"A 76-year-old woman with progressive memory loss can open an envelope and unfold a letter separately but cannot carry out the whole sequence in order. Which deficit is shown?","choices":["Ideomotor apraxia","Ballistic movement","Limb-kinetic apraxia","Choreiform movement","Ideational apraxia"],"answer":4,"why":"Ideational apraxia is inability to organize a sequence though parts are possible; it is typical in diffuse cortical degeneration, for example Alzheimer disease (Motor cortex)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx04","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"A 61-year-old man with a small tumor in the supplementary motor area cannot use the opposite hand skillfully, though strength is preserved. Which deficit best fits?","choices":["Hemiballismus","Ideational apraxia","Limb-kinetic apraxia","Cerebellar tremor","Ideomotor apraxia of both limbs"],"answer":2,"why":"Limb-kinetic apraxia, loss of use of the contralateral hand with preserved strength, follows isolated supplementary motor area lesions (Motor cortex)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx05","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"A 25-year-old man arrives in the emergency department with a racing heart and fast breathing after a weekend binge. Which substance is most likely to have strongly activated his sympathetic system?","choices":["Opioid intoxication","Alcohol intoxication","Cocaine use","Benzodiazepine intoxication","Parasympathetic stimulation"],"answer":2,"why":"Sympathomimetic drugs such as cocaine and amphetamine highly activate the sympathetic system; sedatives do so in withdrawal, not intoxication (Autonomic motor system)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx06","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"A 38-year-old woman has a hypothalamic tumor. Over months she develops a voracious appetite and episodes of rage. Which nucleus is most likely affected?","choices":["Caudate nucleus","Globus pallidus interna","Ventromedial nucleus","Upper lateral nucleus","Subthalamic nucleus"],"answer":2,"why":"Ventromedial nucleus (satiety center) lesions produce voracious appetite and rage (Autonomic motor system)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx07","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"A 45-year-old man with a lesion of the upper lateral hypothalamus loses interest in eating and becomes underweight. Which label fits this region?","choices":["Gatekeeper","Satiety center","Supplementary motor area","Motor strip","Hunger center"],"answer":4,"why":"The hunger center is the upper lateral nucleus; lesions produce profound loss of appetite (Autonomic motor system)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx08","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"A 9-year-old boy touches a hot pan and pulls his hand back before he feels pain. Which description of this response is correct?","choices":["It depends on the cerebellum predicting contraction","It is graded according to motivation","It runs through the corpus callosum","It is local and all-or-none within the spinal cord","It needs association cortex to interpret the stimulus"],"answer":3,"why":"The spinal reflex arc links sensory neuron directly to motor neuron; the response stays local and is all-or-none, without immediate awareness (Primitive reflex circuit)."},
  {"id":"c331-mx09","tag":"Motor cortex and localization","q":"During epilepsy surgery workup, a 30-year-old woman's electrocorticography shows a right parietal discharge spreading to left frontal and right temporal lobes before local parietal spread. What does this best illustrate?","choices":["Brodmann areas each have one fixed function","Primary sensory cortex is unilateral","Seizures never spread across hemispheres","Lesion studies define sufficient structures","Mental functions cannot be naively assigned to one region"],"answer":4,"why":"Such anecdotal electrocorticography evidence shows why a mental function should not be naively pinned to one region (Localization of brain functions)."},
  {"id":"c331-la01","tag":"Language","q":"A 68-year-old right-handed man has a stroke. He understands questions but speaks haltingly and cannot finish a short sentence. The lesion lies where Broca placed loss of fluent speech in 1865. Which region is most likely damaged?","choices":["Right superior temporal lobe","Right inferior frontal lobe","Left superior temporal lobe","Left inferior frontal lobe","Left occipital lobe"],"answer":3,"why":"Broca linked loss of fluent speech to the left inferior frontal lobe; Wernicke placed comprehension in the left superior temporal lobe (Language section)."},
  {"id":"c331-la02","tag":"Language","q":"A 59-year-old woman has a stroke after which she speaks fluently with normal grammar, but she cannot convey or detect emotional tone, and coworkers find her cold. Formal language testing is normal. Which hemisphere is most likely injured?","choices":["Right hemisphere","Left temporal","Left occipital","Brainstem","Left frontal"],"answer":0,"why":"Perceiving and expressing prosody and gestures needs an intact right hemisphere, via a pathway that mirrors the left language pathway; such patients may struggle socially despite intact language."},
  {"id":"c331-la03","tag":"Language","q":"A 34-year-old left-handed man is being planned for neurosurgery and asks how likely it is that his language is on the left. What proportion of left-handers have left-hemispheric language dominance?","choices":["33 percent","67 percent","50 percent","90 percent","99 percent"],"answer":1,"why":"Of left-handers, 67 percent are left-dominant; 33 percent have mixed or right dominance. The 99 percent figure applies to right-handers."},
  {"id":"c331-la04","tag":"Language","q":"A 7-year-old girl of normal intelligence, motivated and well taught, cannot learn to read. Testing shows she cannot tell apart distinct speech sounds. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Persistent vegetative state","Developmental dyslexia","Developmental nonverbal learning disorder","Semantic aphasia","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder"],"answer":1,"why":"Developmental dyslexia is unexpected learning difficulty despite adequate intelligence, motivation and education; failing to tell phonemes apart is the strongest predictor of later reading trouble."},
  {"id":"c331-la05","tag":"Language","q":"A 10-year-old boy has clumsy left-hand fine-motor control, poor visuoperceptual organization, trouble with mathematics and poor peer relationships. Reading is normal. Dysfunction of which region is postulated?","choices":["Left temporal lobe","Right hemisphere","Left inferior frontal gyrus","Occipital lobe bilaterally","Hippocampus"],"answer":1,"why":"Developmental nonverbal learning disorder, with these features, is thought to arise from a poorly working right hemisphere."},
  {"id":"c331-la06","tag":"Language","q":"A 72-year-old woman with nonfluent aphasia after a left hemisphere stroke cannot complete a simple sentence but sings all the words of a hymn at church. What best explains this?","choices":["Nonfluent aphasia never affects word recall","Singing depends on the occipital lobe","Many aspects of music production are in the right hemisphere","Lexical processing is in the right frontal lobe","Semantic processing is spared by all strokes"],"answer":2,"why":"Music is mainly represented in the right hemisphere, so patients with nonfluent aphasia may still sing an entire song."},
  {"id":"c331-la07","tag":"Language","q":"A 63-year-old man with a left temporal tumor can repeat words and phrases accurately but does not understand them and cannot produce speech of his own. Which level of language processing is most likely impaired in isolation?","choices":["Letter identification","Prosody perception","Graphomotor output","Semantic processing","Phonologic processing"],"answer":3,"why":"An isolated semantic defect can leave repetition intact while comprehension and spontaneous speech fail; semantic processing activates the left middle and superior temporal gyri."},
  {"id":"c331-at01","tag":"Arousal and attention","q":"A 45-year-old man has a small hemorrhage in the brainstem reticular formation and is stuporous, though his cerebral hemispheres look normal on imaging. What best explains his depressed alertness?","choices":["Small ARAS lesions can produce stupor","Only bilateral hemispheric lesions cause stupor","The right frontal lobe sets consciousness","Delirium from diffuse cortical disease","Isolated hippocampal injury"],"answer":0,"why":"The ARAS sets the level of consciousness; small discrete ARAS lesions can cause stupor, while hemispheric lesions must be large and bilateral."},
  {"id":"c331-at02","tag":"Arousal and attention","q":"A 30-year-old woman remains months after anoxic injury with extensive permanent bilateral cortical damage. She has sleep-wake cycles and her eyes appear to gaze, but she shows no sign of conscious thought. Which condition is this?","choices":["Persistent vegetative state","Coma from a small ARAS lesion","Delirium","Nonfluent aphasia","Korsakoff syndrome"],"answer":0,"why":"The persistent vegetative state reflects extensive, permanent bilateral cortical dysfunction with the ARAS and thalamus acting alone: sleep-wake cycles and gaze without awareness."},
  {"id":"c331-at03","tag":"Arousal and attention","q":"A 55-year-old man after a head injury is alert and conversant, but on a task requiring him to mark only the letter A in a long random list he stops performing within moments. Which lesion site is most likely?","choices":["Mammillary bodies","Left occipital lobe","Right frontal lobe","Left superior temporal lobe","Medial temporal lobe"],"answer":2,"why":"Maintaining attention on persistence tasks requires an intact right frontal lobe; similar lesions elsewhere usually spare the task."},
  {"id":"c331-at04","tag":"Arousal and attention","q":"An 80-year-old woman with pneumonia becomes acutely confused and cannot maintain a coherent line of thought. Which statement best explains the anatomy of this deficit?","choices":["It reflects isolated mammillary body damage","It reflects loss of right hemisphere prosody","It is diffusely distributed in the cortex, so many medical conditions can disrupt it","It requires a discrete left frontal lesion","It localizes to the planum temporale"],"answer":2,"why":"Keeping thought coherent relies on the cortex diffusely, so a wide range of medical illnesses can upset it and bring on acute confusion or delirium."},
  {"id":"c331-at05","tag":"Arousal and attention","q":"A parent of a 9-year-old boy with ADHD asks what brain scans show in the disorder. Which finding has been reported in functional imaging studies?","choices":["Mammillary body damage","Absent planum temporale asymmetry","Senile plaques in the frontal lobe","Frontal or right hemisphere hypometabolism","Consistent hippocampal atrophy"],"answer":3,"why":"No pathologic finding is consistent in ADHD, but functional imaging has variously shown frontal lobe or right hemisphere hypometabolism compared with controls."},
  {"id":"c331-at06","tag":"Arousal and attention","q":"A neurophysiology student records thalamic and cortical activity in a waking volunteer and then during sleep. Which change is expected as the volunteer falls asleep?","choices":["Bursts become more synchronized","The ARAS fires at 100 cycles per second","Bursts stop entirely","Bursts are no longer synchronized","Only the hippocampus fires"],"answer":3,"why":"Thalamus and cortex fire rhythmic bursts at 20 to 40 cycles per second; in sleep they are not synchronized, and greater synchronization means greater wakefulness."},
  {"id":"c331-me01","tag":"Memory","q":"A 27-year-old man with intractable epilepsy has both medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampi and amygdalae, removed. His seizures stop, but he cannot form or recall new facts, while he can still learn new skills. Which memory system is spared?","choices":["Semantic memory","Episodic memory","Declarative memory","Procedural memory","Recent factual memory"],"answer":3,"why":"H. M. lost memory of facts after bilateral hippocampal and amygdalar removal but kept relatively preserved skill learning, suggesting declarative and procedural memory are separate."},
  {"id":"c331-me02","tag":"Memory","q":"A 66-year-old man with Parkinson disease has trouble learning new motor routines but recalls facts and events well. His motor skill learning improves on levodopa. Which neurotransmitter is postulated to have a role in this memory type?","choices":["Acetylcholine in the basal forebrain only","Dopamine","Serotonin","Glutamate acting on hippocampal place cells","Norepinephrine"],"answer":1,"why":"Nigrostriatal dopamine neurons degenerate in Parkinson disease; because levodopa eases the procedural deficit, dopamine is postulated to play a role in procedural memory."},
  {"id":"c331-me03","tag":"Memory","q":"A 52-year-old man with long-standing heavy alcohol use cannot form new memories and has patchy recall of older events. Damage to which structures is most likely?","choices":["Planum temporale and occipital cortex","Right frontal lobe","Left inferior frontal gyrus","Nigrostriatal tract","Mammillary bodies and dorsal medial thalamic nucleus"],"answer":4,"why":"Korsakoff syndrome follows thiamine deficiency, usually in chronic alcoholism, damaging the dorsal medial thalamic nucleus and mammillary bodies."},
  {"id":"c331-me04","tag":"Memory","q":"A 74-year-old woman has two years of worsening memory, getting lost in familiar places and trouble following complex sentences. Her personality and social conduct are unchanged. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alzheimer disease","Korsakoff syndrome","Persistent vegetative state","Pick disease","Developmental nonverbal learning disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"Alzheimer disease affects parietal and temporal lobes first, giving early memory, syntactical comprehension and visuospatial loss; personality change is a late feature."},
  {"id":"c331-me05","tag":"Memory","q":"A 58-year-old man becomes socially disinhibited and his speech output becomes sparse, yet he understands well and his memory is relatively intact. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alzheimer disease","Korsakoff syndrome","Pick disease","Parkinson disease","Persistent vegetative state"],"answer":2,"why":"Pick disease affects frontal lobes first, so disinhibition and impaired language expression appear early with relatively preserved comprehension and memory."},
  {"id":"c331-me06","tag":"Memory","q":"A 40-year-old woman has a right temporal lobectomy for epilepsy. Lesion studies suggest which type of memory is more likely to be affected, and what is the expected course?","choices":["Procedural memory, permanently lost","Working memory, severely impaired","Remote memory only","Verbal memory, with no compensation","Nonverbal memory, with possible compensation by the left hippocampus"],"answer":4,"why":"Hippocampal function is mildly lateralized: right for nonverbal and left for verbal memories; after a unilateral lesion the opposite hippocampus can take over much of the work."},
  {"id":"c331-me07","tag":"Memory","q":"A 25-year-old pianist is learning a new piece. Early on, imaging shows wide cortical activation including the medial temporal lobe; months later, playing it shows limited activation in premotor and parietal cortex. What is this process called?","choices":["Thalamic synchronization","Hippocampal place coding","Corticalization of motor commands","Lateralization of prosody","Phonologic processing"],"answer":2,"why":"With practice, segments of an act are encoded in premotor and parietal (especially left parietal) cortex and the medial temporal lobe drops out: the corticalization of motor commands."},
  {"id":"c331-me08","tag":"Memory","q":"A 35-year-old woman has bilateral prefrontal contusions after a car crash. She cannot hold a phone number in mind while dialing it. Which memory function is most impaired?","choices":["Procedural memory","Remote memory","Hippocampal place code","Working memory","Semantic word knowledge"],"answer":3,"why":"Working memory stores information for several seconds while it is operated on; clinically, severe impairment needs bilateral prefrontal lesions."},
  {"id":"c331-em01","tag":"Emotion and limbic system","q":"A 72-year-old man has a large right middle cerebral artery stroke. He cannot move his left arm, but when asked, he insists the arm works fine and seems unconcerned. Which term best describes this?","choices":["Hypermetamorphosis","Viscosity","Anosognosia","Witzelsucht","Catastrophic depression"],"answer":2,"why":"Denial of illness, such as inability to move the left hand, after right hemisphere injury is anosognosia (section s331-emo)."},
  {"id":"c331-em02","tag":"Emotion and limbic system","q":"A 58-year-old woman develops severe nonfluent aphasia after a stroke. Within days she becomes tearful and despairing over her loss of speech. Which hemisphere was most likely injured?","choices":["Right hemisphere","Cerebellum","Left hemisphere","Right prefrontal cortex only","Both temporal lobes"],"answer":2,"why":"Severe aphasia from a left hemisphere lesion may set off catastrophic depression once the spared right side grasps the loss (s331-emo)."},
  {"id":"c331-em03","tag":"Emotion and limbic system","q":"A 45-year-old man recovering from a right hemisphere injury no longer laughs at jokes and takes metaphors literally. He also reports that his dreams have lost their imagery. Which further finding best fits the same lesion side?","choices":["Hypersexuality","Loss of the narrative content of dreams","Profound aphasia","Gelastic seizures","Affective disorder"],"answer":4,"why":"Right-sided damage brings affective disorders, loses the visual side of dreams and blunts response to humor and metaphor (s331-emo). Loss of dream narrative and aphasia point to the left side."},
  {"id":"c331-em04","tag":"Emotion and limbic system","q":"A 33-year-old woman survived herpes encephalitis with damage centered on both amygdalae. She correctly names happy, sad and disgusted faces. Which task is she most likely to fail?","choices":["Naming a disgusted face","Recognizing a happy voice","Recognizing fear in a face","Repeating a sentence","Recognizing a sad face"],"answer":2,"why":"Amygdala damage has been reported to remove the ability to recognize fear and anger in faces and voices, with happiness, sadness and disgust possibly preserved (s331-emo)."},
  {"id":"c331-em05","tag":"Emotion and limbic system","q":"A 27-year-old woman with a functional sensory loss shows numbness of one half of her body with no structural cause found. Based on the chapter, which side is more often involved and why?","choices":["Left side, suggesting right hemisphere origin","Right side, from cerebellar origin","Left side, from left hemisphere origin","Both sides equally, from brainstem origin","Right side, from left hemisphere origin"],"answer":0,"why":"Hemisensory conversion changes have repeatedly been noted on the left side of the body more often, suggesting a right hemisphere origin (s331-emo)."},
  {"id":"c331-em06","tag":"Emotion and limbic system","q":"A resident is reviewing a patient with an anterior thalamic lesion and asks which limbic loop, first described in 1937, passes through that nucleus. Which structure directly follows the anterior thalamic nucleus in that loop?","choices":["Nucleus accumbens","Amygdala","Fornix","Cingulate gyrus","Mammillary bodies"],"answer":3,"why":"The Papez circuit runs hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamic nucleus, then cingulate gyrus (s331-emo, Fig. 33-3)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa01","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 40-year-old man with long-standing temporal lobe epilepsy is described by his family as having low sexual interest, intense emotions, and a way of dragging out every conversation that is hard to end. What is this sticky interpersonal style called?","choices":["Viscosity","Anosognosia","Witzelsucht","Hypermetamorphosis","Placidity"],"answer":0,"why":"The proposed TLE personality includes hyposexuality, emotional intensity and a perseverative style termed viscosity (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa02","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 52-year-old woman survived herpes simplex encephalitis with bilateral temporal damage. She is now placid, puts objects in her mouth, makes sexual remarks to staff and keeps switching attention to anything new. What is the most likely syndrome?","choices":["Proposed TLE personality","Anosognosia","Gelastic epilepsy","Klüver-Bucy syndrome","Frontal lobe syndrome"],"answer":3,"why":"Hypersexuality, placidity, oral exploration and hypermetamorphosis after bilateral temporal injury match the Klüver-Bucy picture, the inverse of the TLE personality (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa03","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 61-year-old man has a left prefrontal subcortical infarct. Over the next weeks he becomes depressed and cries without being able to stop. Which lesion would be more likely to cause euphoria and constant punning instead?","choices":["Right prefrontal lesion","Left temporal lesion","Left parietal lesion","Left prefrontal seizure focus","Bilateral amygdala lesion"],"answer":0,"why":"Left prefrontal lesions produce depression and uncontrollable crying, while a comparable right prefrontal lesion may produce laughter, euphoria and witzelsucht (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa04","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 9-year-old boy has brief episodes of sudden laughter without a reason, and EEG shows a left prefrontal focus. What type of seizure is this?","choices":["Grand mal seizure","Gelastic seizure","Psychomotor fear seizure","Atonic seizure","Absence seizure"],"answer":1,"why":"A left prefrontal seizure focus can produce gelastic seizures, where laughter is the ictal event (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa05","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 34-year-old woman with right temporal lobe epilepsy is evaluated between seizures. Which interictal feature would the chapter most expect on her side of the focus?","choices":["Oral exploration","Bland, uniform reactions","Humorless preoccupation with destiny","Excessive emotionality from elation to sadness","Hypermetamorphosis"],"answer":3,"why":"Right TLE may bring excess emotion, swinging between elation and sadness; destiny themes and humorlessness are linked to left TLE (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa06","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 23-year-old man with schizophrenia hears voices during a PET study. Which region would be expected to show decreased activation while he hallucinates?","choices":["Right parietal lobe","Supplementary motor area","Primary auditory cortex","Cerebellar vermis","Primary visual cortex"],"answer":1,"why":"Hallucinating patients activated speech areas including primary auditory cortex but showed decreased activation in speech-monitoring areas: the left middle temporal gyrus and supplementary motor area (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa07","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 19-year-old develops loosening of associations. His mother asks why the illness began now. Which hypothesis does the chapter give for late-teen onset?","choices":["Loss of right hemisphere dominance","Cerebellar degeneration","Peak neuropil density in adolescence","Excess white matter growth","Excessive adolescent synaptic pruning"],"answer":4,"why":"One hypothesis is that excessive synaptic pruning in adolescence leaves too little frontolimbic activity (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fa08","tag":"Fear, aggression, schizophrenia","q":"A 26-year-old woman with schizophrenia has an MRI as part of research. Which finding is most consistent with the reports summarized in the chapter?","choices":["Increased frontal neuropil density","Reduced white matter weight with normal gray matter","Reduced hippocampal and parahippocampal volume","Increased frontal activation during willed action","Enlarged amygdala"],"answer":2,"why":"Pathology and MRI reports describe possibly reduced hippocampal, amygdala and parahippocampal volume; gray, not white, matter weight is reduced and frontal neuropil density is lower (s331-fear)."},
  {"id":"c331-fr01","tag":"Frontal lobes","q":"A 47-year-old man had a meningioma removed with damage to both frontal lobes. His wife says he is slow, irritable, uninterested in things, and withdrawn, but sometimes says crude things without restraint. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Anosognosia","Gelastic epilepsy","Frontal lobe syndrome","Klüver-Bucy syndrome","Proposed TLE personality"],"answer":2,"why":"Slow thinking, poor judgment, low curiosity, withdrawal and irritability, with apathy that can explode into disinhibition, define the frontal lobe syndrome, which tumors can cause (s331-front)."},
  {"id":"c331-fr02","tag":"Frontal lobes","q":"A 38-year-old woman had a head injury. Her family reports poor judgment at work, yet her WAIS-R IQ is average. What best explains the normal IQ?","choices":["IQ depends mainly on the occipital lobes","Frontal lesions always raise IQ","IQ depends mainly on parietal activation","IQ depends mainly on the temporal lobes","IQ depends mainly on the cerebellum"],"answer":2,"why":"Imaging shows IQ relies mostly on parietal activation (left for verbal, right for performance tasks), so frontal pathology may show only in unstructured, stressful real-life settings (s331-front)."},
  {"id":"c331-fr03","tag":"Frontal lobes","q":"A 55-year-old man has a small left frontal infarct on MRI found incidentally. He and his family notice no change in behavior. What best explains this?","choices":["The parietal lobe takes over language","The intact frontal lobe compensates","Only bilateral temporal lesions affect behavior","The lesion must be in white matter","Frontal lobes have no executive role"],"answer":1,"why":"A one-sided frontal lesion may pass unnoticed since the other lobe compensates very efficiently (s331-front)."},
  {"id":"c331-fr04","tag":"Frontal lobes","q":"In a research study, a right-handed man with a right prefrontal lesion is given an ambiguous task. How is he most likely to interpret it?","choices":["With no lateralized pattern","Only by its immediate context","With heightened use of internal memory cues","Through a mirror-image pattern","Entirely through internal drives"],"answer":1,"why":"In right-handed males, right prefrontal lesions removed use of internal associative cues and led to context-bound interpretation; left lesions led to drive-based interpretation (s331-front)."},
  {"id":"c331-fr05","tag":"Frontal lobes","q":"A 30-year-old man with schizophrenia has rambling associations that become more organized on a typical antipsychotic. Which receptor action and site does the chapter link to this effect?","choices":["Serotonin blockade in the occipital lobe","GABA enhancement in the cerebellum","Dopamine release in the amygdala","D2 blockade, with the frontal lobes a possible site","Acetylcholine blockade in the hippocampus"],"answer":3,"why":"Most typical antipsychotics block dopamine at D2 receptors, and the heavily dopamine-innervated frontal lobes may be a significant site of their action (s331-front)."},
  {"id":"c331-dv01","tag":"Neural development","q":"A 19-year-old man has had focal seizures since childhood. MRI shows a cluster of gray matter neurons sitting deep in the white matter, short of the cortex. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":["Neuronal migration","Myelination","Notochord induction","Synaptic pruning","Neural crest formation"],"answer":0,"why":"Neurons that fail to reach the cortex and lodge in ectopic positions form a heterotopia, a migration error shown to cause epilepsy (s331-dev)."},
  {"id":"c331-dv02","tag":"Neural development","q":"A 10-year-old boy has severe dyslexia despite normal intelligence and teaching. His parents ask what brain finding has been reported in this condition. Which finding did a neuropathologic study of four consecutive patients with dyslexia report?","choices":["Persistent subplate neurons in the frontal lobe","Loss of dentate gyrus neurons","Absent neural crest derivatives","Delayed myelination of the corpus callosum","Heterotopias in the planum temporale"],"answer":4,"why":"Heterotopias were a common finding in the planum temporale in the four consecutive dyslexic patients studied (s331-dev)."},
  {"id":"c331-dv03","tag":"Neural development","q":"A medical student asks which embryonic structure becomes the dorsal root ganglia and autonomic ganglia of a 6-week embryo. Which structure gives rise to the peripheral nervous system?","choices":["Neural crest","Neural tube","Proliferative zone","Subplate","Notochord"],"answer":0,"why":"The neural tube becomes the CNS, while the ectoderm just superficial to it becomes the neural crest, which forms the PNS (s331-dev)."},
  {"id":"c331-dv04","tag":"Neural development","q":"A 24-year-old woman with schizophrenia dies in an accident, and her brain is studied. The pathologist finds a transient layer of neurons beneath the cortex that normally disappears during development. What does this finding most likely suggest?","choices":["Failed myelination","Abnormal notochord induction","Failure to complete axonal pathfinding","Overactive synaptic pruning","Excess adult neurogenesis"],"answer":2,"why":"Abnormally persistent subplate neurons in some schizophrenia brains suggest thalamocortical axons did not finish pathfinding; the finding is not present in every case (s331-dev)."},
  {"id":"c331-dv05","tag":"Neural development","q":"A 3-year-old girl's parents ask why toddlers learn so quickly. You explain that the brain forms synapses at its fastest rate during a specific period. When does synaptogenesis peak?","choices":["Ages 10 to 18","First 2 postnatal years","Late in the third decade","Mid-first trimester","Mid-second trimester"],"answer":1,"why":"Synaptogenesis peaks within the first 2 postnatal years, with up to 30 million synapses forming each second (s331-dev)."},
  {"id":"c331-dv06","tag":"Neural development","q":"A neuroscience resident studies why repeatedly used synapses survive pruning in infant cortex. He finds a receptor that admits calcium only when glutamate binds while the membrane is depolarized. Which receptor is this?","choices":["NMDA glutamate receptor","AMPA glutamate receptor","Metabotropic glutamate receptor","GABA-A receptor","Kainate receptor"],"answer":0,"why":"The postsynaptic NMDA glutamate receptor needs both glutamate and depolarization for calcium influx and is thought to mediate synaptic reinforcement (s331-dev)."},
  {"id":"c331-ng01","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 45-year-old man recovering from a traumatic brain injury asks whether adults can grow new neurons. Which region does the chapter name as the main site of adult neurogenesis?","choices":["Planum temporale","Prefrontal cortex","Primary motor cortex","Dentate gyrus of the hippocampus","Cerebellar cortex"],"answer":3,"why":"The chapter names the dentate gyrus of the hippocampus as the main site of adult neurogenesis (s331-neurog). In humans its extent is debated: a 2018 study found none in adults, while a 2025 study found dividing progenitors there (see the update box)."},
  {"id":"c331-ng02","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 32-year-old veteran has years of fear and constant vigilance after a deadly ambush, even at home. A PET scan is done while he recalls the event. Which finding has been reported in such patients?","choices":["Low left hippocampal activity","High right amygdala activity","High prefrontal activity","Low thalamic activity","High cerebellar activity"],"answer":1,"why":"A PET study of PTSD found abnormally high right amygdala activity while patients relived traumatic memories (s331-neurog)."},
  {"id":"c331-ng03","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 7-year-old boy from a violent, chaotic home is hypervigilant and lags in reading and language. His teacher asks for a neurologic explanation. Which mechanism does recent work suggest?","choices":["Excess adult neurogenesis in the hippocampus","Terror flooding the amygdala, favoring threat circuits over language circuits","Arrested neural crest migration","Early completion of myelination","Loss of NMDA receptors in motor cortex"],"answer":1,"why":"Repeated terrifying infant experiences may flood the amygdala and tune memory circuits to threat at the cost of language and academic skills (s331-neurog)."},
  {"id":"c331-ng04","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 30-year-old woman in psychotherapy relives painful childhood memories with a therapist who responds in a safe, accepting way. Her supervisor names this technique. Which concept, and whose, is it?","choices":["Attunement, Bowlby","Free association, Piaget","Separation-individuation, Mahler","Corrective emotional experience, Franz Alexander","Epigenetic stages, Erikson"],"answer":3,"why":"Franz Alexander added reliving early memories in a less pathologic environment, called the corrective emotional experience (s331-neurog)."},
  {"id":"c331-ng05","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 9-month-old's mother responds consistently and sensitively to her baby's emotional expressions, reflecting the baby's feelings back. What is this process called?","choices":["Transference","Corrective emotional experience","Attunement","Synaptic pruning","Tissue induction"],"answer":2,"why":"Attunement is caregivers playing back a child's inner feelings; consistent, sensitive reciprocation reinforces emotional circuits (s331-neurog)."},
  {"id":"c331-ng06","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 26-year-old man has poor social and emotional adjustment. His therapist frames it as a failure to complete an early developmental stage, with later stages built on earlier ones. Which theory is being applied?","choices":["Bowlby's attachment theory","Alexander's corrective emotional experience","Freud's topographic theory","Piaget's cognitive theory","Erikson's epigenetic theory"],"answer":4,"why":"Erikson's epigenetic theory holds that adult behavior depends on sequential completion of childhood stages, and failure shows up as later maladjustment (s331-neurog)."}
]);
